WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.480
The reviews are in for Ang Lee's sci-fi action
film Gemini Man, and they are… surprising.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.690
The film stars Will Smith as a professional
killer who's inexplicably being pursued by

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.740
a younger version of himself and so far, most
critics are saying they enjoyed the visual

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.620
effects but found the story to be severely
underwhelming.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.740
As you may recall, Twitter lit up with praise
for Gemini Man after the film was first screened

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:21.940
for critics in September.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.560
"I make this look good."

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.020
But not so fast.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.670
It looks like most critics who disliked the
film held off on sharing their opinions until

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.300
writing their actual reviews.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.600
And unfortunately, it seems like most critics
fall directly into that category.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:37.640
"No!

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:38.380
No!"

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:40.300
Sadly, yes.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.880
The film which has been in development for
the better part of two decades struck most

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.620
reviewers as being all spectacle and no substance.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.000
This is particularly surprising considering
that Ang Lee directed the film, and he has

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.720
numerous Academy Awards under his belt.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.720
Over the years, he's demonstrated that he
can bring a special, decidedly human touch

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.660
to even the most CGI-heavy blockbuster.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.800
Take 2003's Hulk, for example.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.860
So what went wrong with Gemini Man?

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.100
Judging by the reviews, quite a bit.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.800
Critics say the plot is too thin.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.060
The characters are undercooked.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.300
The dialogue induces groans galore.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.940
And the central gimmick that is, the conflict
between the two Smiths ends up falling rather

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:16.440
flat.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.700
In his two-star review, The Guardian's Peter
Bradshaw writes that,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.120
"The digital novelty is striking for the first
10 minutes, silly for the next 10 minutes,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.800
and by the end of the movie you're pining
for the analogue values of script and direction.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.760
A wittier, smarter riff on everything could
have saved this."

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:34.600
As for Lee's decision to shoot the film at
a high frame rate, Bradshaw writes:

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.720
"[The technique] creates a new pin-sharp clarity
but at the expense of making the film look

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:43.270
like video or like a celluloid movie on a
plasma TV when you haven’t de-activated

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.100
motion smoothing.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.360
It’s not a taste I want to acquire."

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:50.990
Variety's Peter Debruge was keen on reminding
everyone that Gemini Man spent several years

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:55.079
languishing in development hell and he suspects
there's a very good reason for that:

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:58.960
"[Gemini Man was] a nearly impossible project
to get made, […] falling through multiple

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.080
times because the technology wasn't there
yet.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.760
At least, that's been the excuse, although
judging by the finished product, it was the

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.020
script that never lived up to the promise
of its premise."

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.100
If there's a common theme among the film's
many detractors, it's that Gemini Man will

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:17.260
likely be remembered as a fine technical achievement
that ultimately suffers from a supremely half-baked

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:17.760
script.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.900
In the words of CNET's Richard Trenholm:

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.360
"It's genuinely impressive when Smith as we
know him goes face to face and even hand to

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.920
hand with the digitally de-aged mid-20s model.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.820
[...] Unfortunately, it ends up as an unengaging
tech demo, because the characters and storyline

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.500
seem to have been given barely a fraction
of the thought that's gone into the effects."

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.360
Even some of the positive reviews take the
film to task for its lazy writing.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.840
Abbie Bernstein of Assignment X writes that:

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.620
"[The film boasts] what is arguably the best
use of 3D since Avatar.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.160
[...] This makes it all the more disappointing
to report that the screenplay […] is pretty

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.740
standard science-fiction fare.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.120
It's moderately entertaining, but neither
director Lee's visual pizzazz nor the commitment

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.740
of the actors can keep the story from being
predictable and feeling familiar."

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.920
Blackfilm's Wilson Morales agreed:

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.520
"The story is not original but the visual
and action sequences rock.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.460
[...] With so much time given the longevity
to getting greenlit, surely some of the money

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.920
spent on the technology could have gone to
the writers to punch up the script."

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.160
The Hollywood Reporter's Stephen Dalton was
particularly merciless in his review, writing

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:16.660
that

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.160
"The hyper-real look of Gemini Man is immersive
and richly detailed.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.370
But it also has the disconcerting effect of
making a big-budget cinematic spectacle look

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.640
like a vintage videotaped TV drama.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.450
To steal a line from Dolly Parton, it takes
a lot of money to look this cheap."

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.540
"I think I done just got mad."

